meta {
  name: Admin Space Takedown
  type: http
  seq: 165
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/v1/admin/spaces/takedown
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{adminToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "uri": "at://did:plc:example/space/com.example.space/3kabc",
    "applied": true
  }
}

docs {
  # POST /v1/admin/spaces/takedown

  Apply or clear the operator takedown on one Atproto Space — per-space moderation, the
  granularity account takedown cannot reach, and the only action available at all for a
  space whose authority is a foreign server (see `GET /v1/admin/spaces`).

  While a takedown is applied, every space seam answers `SpaceNotFound` for the space:
  reads, writes, `getSpaceCredential`, write-notification fan-out, and the member-facing
  `com.atproto.space.listSpaces`, which omits it. `SpaceNotFound` and not `SpaceDeleted`
  on purpose — `SpaceDeleted` is the spec's durable "drop your copy" signal to syncers,
  and a takedown is reversible. It is also the reply an unknown space gets, so which
  spaces an operator refuses is not disclosed.

  Nothing is destroyed: the simplespace config, member list, notification registrations,
  and every stored repo are left exactly as they were, so clearing the takedown returns
  the space to its prior state. The URI also cannot be re-created around the takedown —
  `createSpace` refuses to claim a taken-down row.

  Admin-authed: the master token (shown here) **or** an active companion-app device's
  signed request. The URI travels in the signed body, so a takedown signature is bound
  to its space.

  Body:
  - `uri` — canonical space URI, `at://{authority}/space/{type}/{skey}`.
  - `applied` — `true` applies the takedown, `false` clears it (the `takedown.applied`
    shape `com.atproto.admin.updateSubjectStatus` uses for an account).

  Response (the resulting state as the row holds it, not an echo of the request):
  - `uri` — the space URI.
  - `applied` — whether a takedown is now in force.
  - `takendownAt` — when it started; omitted once cleared. Re-applying an existing
    takedown keeps the original timestamp.

  Idempotent in both directions. A URI this server stores nothing for is a 404 — it
  cannot refuse to serve a space it does not have. Checked after auth, so there is no
  space-presence oracle.
}
